<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shango</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ṣàngó, Xangô, Changó</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shango is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> of fire, thunder, lightning, drumming, dance, strength and justice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion. Tradition holds that he was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafin</a>, or king, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a> and Ajaka, a warlike ruler whose seven-year reign ended when lightning destroyed his palace, and who was deified after his death; his wives are named as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oshun">Oshun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> and Oya. He is known for his double-headed axe, the oṣé. Enslaved Yoruba carried his worship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where as Xangô in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> and Changó in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a> he is identified with Saint Barbara or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Saint Jerome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shango.jpg" alt="Shango" width="330" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dornicke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Representação_de_Xangô_MN_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Oyo Empire, which from the 17th century dominated much of the Yoruba country and the lands to the west, spread the worship of <b>Shango</b>, the orisha of thunder, remembered in tradition as an early king of Oyo.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
